(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[28] = {"n":28,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 20% · B2 50% · B2+ 30%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Asteroid mining","p":["Between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter lies the asteroid belt, a region containing millions of rocky and metallic objects left over from the formation of the solar system about 4.6 billion years ago. Other asteroids travel in orbits that bring them relatively close to Earth. For decades, these objects interested mainly astronomers. Today, however, a growing number of scientists, engineers and companies believe that asteroids could one day become a source of valuable resources, an idea known as asteroid mining.","Asteroids vary greatly in composition. Some are rich in carbon and contain water locked in minerals. Others are made largely of iron and nickel, and some may contain significant quantities of rare and valuable metals, such as platinum, which are used in electronics, medical devices and industrial processes. One metallic asteroid, called Psyche, is so large and rich in metal that some commentators have estimated its theoretical value at far more than the entire world economy, although such figures are highly speculative and ignore the practical difficulties of extracting and selling the metal.","In the near future, the most valuable resource in space may not be metal but water. Water is extremely heavy, and launching it from Earth is very expensive. If water could be extracted from asteroids or from the Moon, it could be used for drinking and growing food on long missions, and it could also be split into hydrogen and oxygen, which are the main components of rocket fuel. Fuel depots in space could therefore make exploration of the solar system much cheaper, in the same way that refuelling stations made long-distance travel possible on Earth.","Several space missions have already visited asteroids and returned samples to Earth. Japan's Hayabusa2 mission collected material from the asteroid Ryugu and returned it in 2020, while NASA's OSIRIS-REx mission brought back samples from the asteroid Bennu in 2023. These missions have provided valuable scientific information about the early solar system and have shown that it is possible to land on, or touch, an asteroid and collect material. However, the amounts collected were tiny, measured in grams, rather than the tonnes that commercial mining would require.","The challenges facing asteroid mining are enormous. Asteroids have very weak gravity, so mining equipment would have to be anchored to prevent it from floating away. The machines would need to operate largely by themselves, since radio signals can take many minutes to travel between Earth and a distant asteroid. Costs would be huge, and it is uncertain whether bringing metals back to Earth could ever be profitable, especially since a sudden increase in supply might cause prices to fall. Several private companies that were founded in the 2010s to pursue asteroid mining later ran out of money or changed their focus.","There are also legal and ethical questions. The Outer Space Treaty of 1967 states that no nation can claim ownership of the Moon or other celestial bodies, but it does not clearly address whether resources taken from them can be owned. Some countries have passed national laws allowing their companies to keep resources they extract, while others argue that space resources should benefit all humanity. As technology develops, international agreement on these issues will become increasingly important. For now, asteroid mining remains a fascinating possibility rather than a practical industry."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the formation of the planet Mars","how rockets are launched","the history of the Moon landings","the possibilities and challenges of mining asteroids"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage discusses asteroid mining.","h":"التعدين في الكويكبات."},{"n":2,"s":"What does the writer suggest about the estimated value of Psyche?","o":["It is an accurate measurement.","It has already been earned.","It is unrealistic because it ignores practical difficulties.","It is smaller than the value of water."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"'highly speculative'.","h":"تقديرات غير واقعية."},{"n":3,"s":"Why might water be the most valuable resource in space?","o":["It is rare on Earth.","It can be sold for high prices on Earth.","It protects spacecraft from asteroids.","It is expensive to launch from Earth and can be used for fuel."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"مكلف الإطلاق ويُستخدم وقودًا."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the writer compare fuel depots in space to refuelling stations on Earth?","o":["to show that space travel is like driving","to explain how fuel is made","to show how they could make long journeys cheaper","to criticize the cost of petrol"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"The analogy explains their usefulness.","h":"تشبيه لتوضيح الفائدة."},{"n":5,"s":"What did the Hayabusa2 and OSIRIS-REx missions show?","o":["Asteroid mining is already profitable.","Asteroids contain no water.","It is possible to collect material from an asteroid.","Asteroids are too dangerous to approach."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"إمكانية جمع العينات."},{"n":6,"s":"The word 'anchored' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["painted","firmly attached","removed","repaired"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Fixed so that it does not float away.","h":"مثبت."},{"n":7,"s":"Why would mining machines need to operate largely by themselves?","o":["Humans are not allowed in space.","The machines are too small to control.","Asteroids have strong gravity.","Radio signals take a long time to travel between Earth and asteroids."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"تأخر الإشارات."},{"n":8,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'it' in 'to prevent it from floating away' refers to","o":["mining equipment","gravity","the asteroid","the radio signal"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"The equipment might float away.","h":"تعود على المعدات."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge for asteroid mining?","o":["weak gravity","high costs","uncertain profitability","a shortage of trained astronauts"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Astronauts are not mentioned.","h":"نقص رواد الفضاء غير مذكور."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Asteroid mining will begin next year.","Asteroid mining has been banned.","Asteroid mining is cheaper than mining on Earth.","Asteroid mining is still a possibility rather than a real industry."],"k":3,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final sentence.","h":"احتمال لا صناعة قائمة."}]},{"t":"The Library of Alexandria","p":["Few institutions of the ancient world have inspired as much admiration, or as many legends, as the Library of Alexandria. Founded in the early third century BCE in the Egyptian city of Alexandria, it was intended to collect all the knowledge of the known world. For several centuries, it was one of the most important centres of scholarship anywhere, and its fate has become a powerful symbol of the fragility of knowledge.","Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BCE. After his death, Egypt was ruled by one of his generals, Ptolemy, and his successors. The early Ptolemaic kings were ambitious patrons of learning. They established the Mouseion, a research institution dedicated to the Muses, the goddesses of the arts and sciences, and the library was part of this complex. Scholars from across the Mediterranean world were invited to live and work there, receiving salaries, accommodation and access to the collection.","The Ptolemies were determined to acquire books by any means. According to ancient sources, ships arriving in Alexandria's harbour were searched for written works, which were copied. The library kept the originals and returned the copies to their owners. One famous story claims that Ptolemy III borrowed the official texts of the great Athenian playwrights, paying a large deposit, and then chose to keep the originals and lose the deposit. Estimates of the size of the collection vary widely, from tens of thousands to several hundred thousand scrolls, and modern historians treat the larger figures with caution.","Remarkable scholarship took place in Alexandria. Euclid is believed to have worked there when he wrote his influential book on geometry. Eratosthenes, who served as head librarian, calculated the circumference of the Earth with impressive accuracy by comparing the angle of the sun's rays in two different cities. Scholars also produced careful editions of earlier literature, comparing manuscripts to remove errors, and developed methods of organizing and cataloguing knowledge.","How the library came to an end is one of history's great puzzles. A popular story claims that it was destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. In fact, the evidence suggests a gradual decline over several centuries. In 48 BCE, during a civil war, a fire started by Julius Caesar's forces may have destroyed some books, possibly in warehouses near the harbour. Royal support decreased under later rulers, scholars were expelled during political conflicts, and further destruction occurred during wars in the third century CE. By the time of later events sometimes blamed for its destruction, the great library had probably long since ceased to exist in its original form. Many historians regard a frequently repeated story connecting its destruction to the Muslim conquest of Egypt as a legend that appeared centuries later and has little reliable evidence to support it.","In 2002, a new library, the Bibliotheca Alexandrina, opened near the site of the ancient one, with the support of UNESCO and many countries. Its striking circular building, which slopes towards the sea, houses millions of books, as well as museums and research centres. The story of the ancient library reminds us that knowledge depends not only on collecting books but also on continued investment, peaceful conditions and the preservation of records for future generations."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Military Campaigns of Alexander","How Ships Were Built in Egypt","The Rise and Decline of a Great Ancient Library","The Life of Julius Caesar"],"k":2,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage traces the library's history.","h":"صعود المكتبة وأفولها."},{"n":12,"s":"What was the Mouseion?","o":["a research institution of which the library was part","a temple for sailors","a royal palace","a type of scroll"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"مؤسسة بحثية."},{"n":13,"s":"What does the story about Ptolemy III suggest?","o":["Athens refused to lend its texts.","The library was very poor.","Ptolemy III disliked plays.","The rulers were extremely determined to obtain books."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He gave up a deposit to keep the originals.","h":"الإصرار على اقتناء الكتب."},{"n":14,"s":"Why do modern historians treat the larger estimates of the collection 'with caution'?","o":["The figures in ancient sources vary widely and may be exaggerated.","All the scrolls have been counted.","The library never contained scrolls.","Ancient sources give no figures."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Estimates vary from tens to hundreds of thousands.","h":"تفاوت التقديرات."},{"n":15,"s":"How did Eratosthenes calculate the Earth's circumference?","o":["by sailing around the world","by measuring the length of the Nile","by comparing the angle of the sun's rays in two cities","by reading earlier books"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"مقارنة زاوية أشعة الشمس."},{"n":16,"s":"The word 'catastrophic' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["extremely destructive","slightly harmful","carefully planned","easily controlled"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"A disastrous fire.","h":"كارثي."},{"n":17,"s":"According to the passage, how did the library most probably come to an end?","o":["in a single great fire","through gradual decline over several centuries","by a sudden earthquake","by being moved to Rome"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"تراجع تدريجي."},{"n":18,"s":"What does the writer say about the story linking the library's destruction to the Muslim conquest?","o":["It is confirmed by many ancient sources.","It explains the fire of 48 BCE.","It was written by Eratosthenes.","Many historians consider it a later legend without reliable evidence."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 5 states this.","h":"أسطورة متأخرة."},{"n":19,"s":"In paragraph 6, the word 'Its' in 'Its striking circular building' refers to","o":["UNESCO","the Bibliotheca Alexandrina","the ancient library","the sea"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"The new library's building.","h":"تعود على المكتبة الجديدة."},{"n":20,"s":"What lesson does the writer draw from the library's history?","o":["Preserving knowledge requires ongoing support and peaceful conditions.","Large libraries are unnecessary.","Books should only be kept in one place.","Ancient knowledge is no longer useful."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the last sentence.","h":"الحفاظ على المعرفة يتطلب الدعم."}]},{"t":"Geothermal energy","p":["Beneath our feet lies an enormous store of heat. The Earth's core is estimated to be about as hot as the surface of the Sun, and heat flows constantly outwards towards the surface. Much of this heat comes from the slow radioactive decay of elements in the rocks of the Earth's interior. Geothermal energy is the use of this natural heat to generate electricity or to warm buildings, and unlike solar and wind power, it is available twenty-four hours a day, regardless of the weather.","Humans have used geothermal heat for thousands of years. Hot springs were used for bathing and cooking in ancient China, and the Romans built elaborate baths fed by natural hot water. The first geothermal power station was built in 1904 at Larderello, in Italy, where steam from underground was used to drive a generator. Larderello still produces electricity today.","Most geothermal power stations are located in regions where hot rock lies relatively close to the surface, usually near the boundaries of tectonic plates or in volcanic areas. In these places, underground water is heated by the rocks and may reach temperatures of over two hundred degrees Celsius. Engineers drill wells to bring the hot water or steam to the surface, where it drives turbines that generate electricity. The cooled water is then usually pumped back underground to be reheated, making the system largely renewable. Iceland, which sits on a plate boundary, produces a large share of its electricity from geothermal sources and heats most of its homes with geothermal water. Kenya, Indonesia, the Philippines and New Zealand are also major users.","Geothermal energy has several advantages. It produces very low greenhouse gas emissions compared with fossil fuels, and power stations occupy relatively little land. Because it provides a steady supply of power, it can complement solar and wind energy, whose output varies. Once a plant has been built, operating costs are relatively low, since the 'fuel' is free.","However, there are also disadvantages. Suitable sites are limited, and drilling deep wells is expensive and risky, since developers cannot always be sure how much heat they will find. Geothermal fluids can contain harmful gases, such as hydrogen sulphide, which smells of rotten eggs, and dissolved minerals that can damage equipment. In some cases, injecting water deep underground has triggered small earthquakes, which has led to the cancellation of certain projects.","New technologies may expand the use of geothermal energy. Enhanced geothermal systems create artificial reservoirs by pumping water into hot, dry rock to open small cracks, allowing heat to be extracted in places without natural hot water. Geothermal heat pumps, meanwhile, use the stable temperature just a few metres below the surface to heat buildings in winter and cool them in summer, and they can be used almost anywhere. Saudi Arabia also has geothermal potential, particularly along the Red Sea coast and in volcanic areas in the west of the country, where researchers have studied hot springs and underground temperatures as part of efforts to diversify the Kingdom's energy sources."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of Roman baths","how geothermal energy works and its advantages and limitations","how earthquakes are caused","why Iceland is cold"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains geothermal energy.","h":"الطاقة الحرارية الأرضية."},{"n":22,"s":"What advantage does geothermal energy have over solar and wind power?","o":["It is available at all times regardless of the weather.","It is cheaper to build.","It can be used everywhere.","It produces no gases at all."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"متاحة دائمًا."},{"n":23,"s":"Why does the writer mention Larderello?","o":["as the site of the first geothermal power station","as an example of a failed project","to describe ancient Roman baths","to show where earthquakes occur"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Built in 1904 and still working.","h":"أول محطة."},{"n":24,"s":"Why are most geothermal power stations located near plate boundaries or volcanic areas?","o":["Land is cheaper there.","There is more wind there.","Hot rock lies relatively close to the surface there.","Governments require it."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"قرب الصخور الساخنة."},{"n":25,"s":"Why is the cooled water pumped back underground?","o":["to prevent earthquakes","to clean the turbines","to supply drinking water","so that it can be reheated, making the system largely renewable"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"لإعادة تسخينه."},{"n":26,"s":"The word 'complement' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["replace completely","compete with","work well together with","reduce"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"It supports variable sources.","h":"يكمل."},{"n":27,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a disadvantage of geothermal energy?","o":["Suitable sites are limited.","Drilling is expensive and risky.","It produces large amounts of noise pollution.","It can trigger small earthquakes."],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Noise is not mentioned.","h":"الضوضاء غير مذكورة."},{"n":28,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'they' in 'how much heat they will find' refers to","o":["developers","wells","gases","minerals"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"Developers cannot be sure.","h":"تعود على المطورين."},{"n":29,"s":"How do enhanced geothermal systems differ from conventional ones?","o":["They use heat from the Sun.","They create artificial reservoirs in hot, dry rock.","They only work in Iceland.","They do not use water at all."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 6 explains this.","h":"خزانات صناعية."},{"n":30,"s":"What can be inferred about geothermal energy in Saudi Arabia?","o":["It already provides most of the Kingdom's electricity.","It is impossible because there are no volcanic areas.","It has been rejected by researchers.","It could contribute to a more varied energy mix."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"It is studied to diversify energy sources.","h":"إمكانية التنويع."}]},{"t":"Mirror neurons","p":["In the early 1990s, a team of neuroscientists at the University of Parma in Italy, led by Giacomo Rizzolatti, was studying the brains of macaque monkeys. The researchers were recording the activity of individual nerve cells in an area of the brain involved in planning movements, such as reaching for and grasping objects. They noticed something unexpected: certain cells became active not only when a monkey picked up a piece of food, but also when it watched a researcher pick one up. The researchers called these cells 'mirror neurons', because they seemed to reflect the actions of others in the observer's own brain.","The discovery attracted enormous attention. If the brain automatically simulates the actions of others, the researchers suggested, mirror neurons might help explain how we understand what other people are doing and why. Rather than having to reason consciously about someone's behaviour, we might understand it partly by internally 'rehearsing' it. Further experiments showed that some mirror neurons responded differently depending on the purpose of an action, for example whether an object was grasped in order to eat it or to place it in a container, suggesting a link to understanding intentions.","Studying single neurons in humans is rarely possible, since it requires placing electrodes inside the brain. However, brain imaging studies have found that regions involved in performing actions also become active when people observe them. In one rare study of patients who had electrodes implanted for medical reasons, researchers did find individual cells with mirror-like properties. Many scientists therefore believe that humans possess a mirror system, a network of brain regions with similar functions.","Over the following years, enthusiastic researchers and journalists proposed that mirror neurons might explain a remarkable range of human abilities, including imitation, language, empathy and even culture. Some suggested that problems with the mirror system might explain difficulties in social interaction experienced by some autistic people. Popular articles described mirror neurons as the cells that 'made us human'.","More recently, many neuroscientists have argued that these claims went far beyond the evidence. Critics point out that understanding actions can also depend on other brain areas and on knowledge gained through experience. Monkeys have mirror neurons, yet they do not have language or human-like culture, so mirror neurons alone cannot explain these abilities. Studies testing the idea of a 'broken mirror' in autism have produced mixed results, and many researchers no longer consider it a convincing explanation. Some scientists have even suggested that mirror neurons may develop as a result of learning, through repeatedly seeing and performing the same actions, rather than being a built-in system for understanding others.","The story of mirror neurons offers a useful lesson about how science works. An important discovery can generate exciting hypotheses, but these must be tested carefully, and early enthusiasm is sometimes followed by a more balanced view. Mirror neurons remain a genuine and interesting finding, and research on them continues. However, most scientists now see them as one part of a complex system that allows us to understand others, rather than as a single key to the human mind."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the discovery of mirror neurons and debates about their importance","how monkeys learn to use tools","the history of the University of Parma","how to treat brain injuries"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage traces discovery and debate.","h":"الخلايا العصبية المرآتية."},{"n":32,"s":"Why were these cells called 'mirror neurons'?","o":["They were found near the eyes.","They reflected light.","They became active both when acting and when watching others act.","They only worked in front of mirrors."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"تعكس أفعال الآخرين."},{"n":33,"s":"What did the experiments about the purpose of actions suggest?","o":["Mirror neurons may be linked to understanding intentions.","Monkeys cannot recognize food.","Mirror neurons respond to all actions in the same way.","Mirror neurons only work during sleep."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Responses varied with the purpose.","h":"فهم النوايا."},{"n":34,"s":"Why is it rarely possible to study single neurons in humans?","o":["Human neurons are too small to see.","It requires placing electrodes inside the brain.","Humans have no mirror neurons.","It is forbidden in Italy."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"يتطلب أقطابًا داخل الدماغ."},{"n":35,"s":"The word 'enthusiastic' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["doubtful","careless","experienced","very eager and excited"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Researchers were very excited.","h":"متحمس."},{"n":36,"s":"Why does the writer mention that monkeys have mirror neurons but no language?","o":["to prove that monkeys are intelligent","to show that mirror neurons alone cannot explain language","to suggest that monkeys will learn language","to describe how monkeys communicate"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It challenges the broad claims.","h":"دحض المبالغات."},{"n":37,"s":"What is the current view of the 'broken mirror' theory of autism?","o":["It has been fully proven.","Many researchers no longer find it convincing.","It is the only accepted explanation.","It has never been tested."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Studies produced mixed results.","h":"غير مقنعة."},{"n":38,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'it' in 'no longer consider it a convincing explanation' refers to","o":["language","culture","the idea of a 'broken mirror' in autism","the brain"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"It refers to the broken mirror idea.","h":"تعود على فرضية المرآة المكسورة."},{"n":39,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It compares two universities.","It describes a discovery, the claims it inspired and later criticism.","It gives instructions for an experiment.","It lists the parts of the brain."],"k":1,"ty":"organization","e":"The paragraphs follow this order.","h":"اكتشاف ثم ادعاءات ثم نقد."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards mirror neurons?","o":["They explain everything about humans.","They are interesting but have been overstated.","They do not exist at all.","They are of no scientific interest."],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The final paragraph presents this view.","h":"مهمة لكن مبالغ فيها."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Nowhere else in the region ______ such a variety of birds.","o":["you can find","you find","find you","can you find"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"can you find — Nowhere at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Nowhere."},{"n":42,"s":"If the bridge hadn't collapsed last year, the journey ______ much shorter now.","o":["would have been","will be","would be","is"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would be — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ to the conditions of the desert, the plant needs very little water.","o":["Adapting","Adapted","To adapt","Having adapting"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Adapted — Past participle clause.","h":"اسم المفعول."},{"n":44,"s":"The board insisted that the director ______ the report personally.","o":["presents","present","presenting","to present"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"present — Subjunctive after insist that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"The thieves are believed ______ the country before the police arrived.","o":["to have left","to leave","leaving","that left"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have left — Passive reporting about the past.","h":"are believed to have."},{"n":46,"s":"If only we ______ more time to visit the museums yesterday!","o":["had","have had","would have","had had"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had had — If only + past perfect.","h":"If only."},{"n":47,"s":"It was her determination ______ impressed the interview panel most.","o":["what","that","who","which it"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"that — It was … that.","h":"جملة التوكيد."},{"n":48,"s":"In no way ______ responsible for the delay; the supplier was.","o":["the driver was","did the driver","was the driver","the driver"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was the driver — In no way: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد In no way."},{"n":49,"s":"The researchers tested six new drugs, none of ______ proved effective.","o":["whom","them","which","that"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — none of which for things.","h":"none of which."},{"n":50,"s":"______ you change your mind, just let me know.","o":["Should","Would","Had","Were"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Should — Should at the start = if.","h":"Should بدل If."},{"n":51,"s":"When the firefighters arrived, the fire ______ for over an hour.","o":["has been burning","was burned","had been burning","burns"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been burning — Past perfect continuous.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"The singer hates ______ by photographers wherever she goes.","o":["following","to follow","be followed","being followed"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"being followed — hate + passive gerund.","h":"hate + being."},{"n":53,"s":"The new students had difficulty ______ to the hot climate.","o":["to adjust","adjusting","adjust","for adjust"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"adjusting — have difficulty + -ing.","h":"difficulty + -ing."},{"n":54,"s":"I'll lend you my car ______ you drive carefully.","o":["unless","even if","whereas","on condition that"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"on condition that — on condition that.","h":"on condition that."},{"n":55,"s":"It's time the children ______ to bed; it's nearly ten o'clock.","o":["went","go","will go","have gone"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"went — It's time + past.","h":"It's time + الماضي."},{"n":56,"s":"The results of the competition ______ on the website next Monday.","o":["will be published","will publish","are publishing","have published"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will be published — Future passive.","h":"المستقبل المبني للمجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ the difficult conditions, the team completed the climb.","o":["Although","However","Whereas","Notwithstanding"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Notwithstanding — Notwithstanding + noun (formal).","h":"Notwithstanding."},{"n":58,"s":"The doctor persuaded my grandfather ______ smoking.","o":["giving up","give up","to give up","that give up"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to give up — persuade + object + to-infinitive.","h":"persuade sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The less you sleep, ______ you can concentrate.","o":["less","the least","the less","the fewer"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the less — Double comparative with an uncountable idea.","h":"the less."},{"n":60,"s":"The new employee is capable ______ handling several tasks at once.","o":["of","to","for","in"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"of — capable of + -ing.","h":"capable of."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) scientists (B) has (C) published their (D) findings.","o":["scientists","has","published","findings"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"has — Plural subject: have.","h":"الفاعل جمع."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) avoids (B) to drive (C) during rush (D) hour.","o":["avoids","to drive","during","hour"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to drive — avoid + -ing: driving.","h":"avoid + -ing."},{"n":63,"s":"(A) Unless you don't hurry, you (B) will (C) miss the (D) bus.","o":["Unless","will","miss","bus"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Unless — Unless already means 'if not': If you don't hurry.","h":"Unless لا تحتاج نفيًا."},{"n":64,"s":"This is the (A) most (B) beautiful place I (C) have ever (D) visit.","o":["most","beautiful","have","visit"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"visit — Present perfect: visited.","h":"visited."},{"n":65,"s":"We (A) were (B) surprised by (C) how (D) quick the work was finished.","o":["were","surprised","how","quick"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"quick — An adverb is needed: quickly.","h":"الظرف quickly."},{"n":66,"s":"The man (A) whom I thought (B) was my neighbour (C) turned out to be a (D) stranger.","o":["whom","was","turned","stranger"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"whom — Subject of 'was': who.","h":"who فاعل."},{"n":67,"s":"He (A) has been (B) living here (C) for (D) he was born.","o":["has","living","for","he was born"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"for — since + point in time.","h":"since."},{"n":68,"s":"(A) Hardly (B) never (C) does he arrive (D) late.","o":["Hardly","never","does","late"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"never — Double negative: ever.","h":"Hardly ever."},{"n":69,"s":"The film was (A) so (B) bored that many people (C) left (D) early.","o":["so","bored","left","early"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"bored — -ing adjective: boring.","h":"boring."},{"n":70,"s":"I (A) would appreciate it if you (B) could (C) send me the (D) informations.","o":["would","could","send","informations"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"informations — information is uncountable.","h":"information غير معدود."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M28-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the second man really mean by 'a slight problem'?","o":["It was a minor issue.","It was not a mistake at all.","It was a very serious mistake.","It was the director's fault."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"He admits he was being ironic.","h":"خطأ جسيم."}],"t":"An understatement about a huge mistake","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking in an office. (64 words)","Man: Did you hear what happened with the email to the clients?","Man: Yes. Somebody sent the confidential price list to all two thousand customers instead of the managers. I'd say that was a slight problem.","Man: A slight problem? The director has been on the phone all morning apologizing.","Man: I know. I was being ironic. It's probably the biggest mistake this company has made in years."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M28-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What can be inferred about the mother's opinion of the scarf?","o":["She wears it every day.","She probably did not like it.","She lost it.","She thought it was too expensive."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"She has never worn it.","h":"لم يعجبها."}],"t":"A hint that a gift was not liked","sc":["Situation: Two sisters are talking. (54 words)","Woman: Did Mum like the scarf we gave her for Eid?","Woman: Well, she said it was very unusual and that she'd keep it for a special occasion.","Woman: Has she worn it yet?","Woman: Not once, and that was four months ago. I think next year we should ask her what she'd like before we buy anything."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M28-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What is the supervisor's attitude to the second experiment?","o":["He refuses completely.","He agrees, but with a condition.","He is extremely enthusiastic.","He wants to do it himself."],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"He sets a deadline condition.","h":"موافقة مشروطة."}],"t":"A supervisor's cautious approval","sc":["Situation: A researcher speaks to her supervisor. (70 words)","Woman: Dr. Majed, I'd like to add a second experiment to my project, testing the effect of temperature as well.","Man: It's an interesting idea, and I'm not against it. But you have only four months left. Can you realistically finish both?","Woman: I think so, if I start next week.","Man: All right, go ahead, but if the first experiment isn't finished by the end of next month, we'll drop the second one."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M28-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What does the first man want?","o":["to borrow some money","to take the bus together","a lift home","directions to the library"],"k":2,"ty":"function","e":"He hints about the bus strike.","h":"توصيلة."}],"t":"A friend's indirect request for a lift","sc":["Situation: Two friends are leaving a lecture. (57 words)","Man: Are you driving home after this?","Man: Yes, why?","Man: Oh, no reason. It's just that the buses are on strike today, and taxis are almost impossible to find. And I live quite near you, as you know.","Man: Ha, I get the hint. Come on, I'll give you a lift. My car's in the car park behind the library."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M28-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"How does the second woman apologize?","o":["unwillingly","very warmly","immediately and sincerely","by email"],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'all right, I suppose … Sorry.'","h":"اعتذار على مضض."}],"t":"A reluctant apology","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking after a meeting. (54 words)","Woman: You interrupted me three times during my presentation. It was quite embarrassing.","Woman: Well, I just wanted to correct a few figures. But … all right, I suppose I could have waited until the end. Sorry.","Woman: Thank you. Next time, could you send me your comments privately?","Woman: Fine. I'll do that from now on, I promise."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M28-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What does the second man mean by 'a piece of cake'?","o":["The assignment was very long.","He was hungry while working.","The assignment was confusing.","The assignment was easy."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"It was similar to lab exercises.","h":"سهل جدًا."}],"t":"A comment that a task is a piece of cake","sc":["Situation: Two students are talking about an assignment. (58 words)","Man: Have you started the computer programming assignment? I'm really worried about it.","Man: I finished it last night. Honestly, it was a piece of cake. It's very similar to the exercises we did in the lab.","Man: Really? Could you show me how you approached the second question?","Man: Sure. Let's meet in the library at two, and I'll explain it."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M28-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What is the manager trying to do?","o":["start a new discussion","end the meeting","discuss parking immediately","extend the meeting"],"k":1,"ty":"function","e":"She mentions the time and thanks everyone.","h":"إنهاء الاجتماع."}],"t":"A hint that a meeting should end","sc":["Situation: A manager speaks during a long meeting. (58 words)","Woman: Well, we've been here for nearly two hours, and I'm sure everyone has other work waiting.","Man: There was one more point I wanted to raise, about the parking arrangements.","Woman: Perhaps you could send that by email, and we'll add it to next week's agenda. Thank you all for your contributions today.","Man: Of course. I'll send it this afternoon."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M28-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What is the second man's opinion of the plan?","o":["It is too ambitious.","It is perfectly organized.","It is too expensive.","It is too boring. Longer conversations"],"k":0,"ty":"opinion","e":"He suggests fewer cities.","h":"خطة مزدحمة."}],"t":"Doubts about a travel plan","sc":["Situation: Two friends are discussing a trip. (67 words)","Man: My plan is to visit five cities in Europe in seven days.","Man: Five cities in a week? That sounds exhausting. You'll spend most of your time on trains and in airports.","Man: But I want to see as much as possible.","Man: I understand, but you might enjoy it more if you chose two or three cities and spent more time in each. You'd actually see more that way."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M28-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why does the second woman say they should be careful with the airport data?","o":["The data is not official.","The records are incomplete.","Airports do not measure temperature.","Urban growth around the stations may have increased temperatures."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"She explains the heat island effect.","h":"تأثير الجزر الحرارية."},{"n":80,"s":"Why do they want to compare the city data with a rural station?","o":["because rural stations are more modern","to find the hottest place in the country","to separate the effect of urban growth from wider warming","because their professor requires it"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"Less rural warming would indicate urban effects.","h":"عزل أثر التوسع العمراني."}],"t":"Discussing data for a climate project","sc":["Situation: Two students are working on a project. (183 words)","Woman: For our project on rising temperatures in Saudi cities, I've found temperature records for Riyadh, Jeddah and Dammam going back to 1980.","Woman: That's great. Where are they from?","Woman: The national meteorological centre. They're official measurements from airport weather stations.","Woman: Good, that's reliable. But we should be careful. Airports are often surrounded by roads and buildings, which absorb heat. Part of the warming might be caused by the cities growing around the stations, not just by climate change.","Woman: The urban heat island effect. I hadn't thought of that.","Woman: We could compare the city data with a station in a rural area, if there's one with records for the same period. If the rural station shows less warming, the difference might be due to urban growth.","Woman: That's a clever approach. I'll check whether there's a station near Al-Ula or somewhere similar.","Woman: We also need to decide how to present the data. A line graph for each city would show the trends clearly.","Woman: And we could add a table with the average temperature for each decade.","Woman: Perfect. Let's aim to finish the analysis by next Tuesday."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M28-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What problem happened at last year's career fair?","o":["The hall was overcrowded at midday but empty in the afternoon.","Too few companies attended.","The fair was cancelled.","Students were not allowed to bring CVs."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated by the second man.","h":"الازدحام وقت الظهيرة."},{"n":82,"s":"Why do they decide to recommend times rather than make them compulsory?","o":["Students refused to follow schedules.","Some companies want students from several faculties.","The hall is too small.","The careers office disagreed."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Explained in the conversation.","h":"الشركات تريد طلابًا من كليات متعددة."}],"t":"Planning a career fair","sc":["Situation: Two members of a student committee are planning an event. (173 words)","Man: We've confirmed twenty-five companies for the career fair next month. Now we need to plan the day itself.","Man: Last year, the main complaint was that the hall was too crowded around midday, while it was almost empty in the afternoon.","Man: Maybe we could schedule visits by faculty. Engineering students in the morning, business students around midday, and so on.","Man: That's a good idea, but some companies want students from several faculties. Perhaps we could just recommend times rather than make them compulsory.","Man: Fair enough. What about preparing students? Many come without a CV.","Man: The careers office could run CV workshops the week before. We could also set up a station at the fair where volunteers check CVs.","Man: And we need a map of the stands. Last year, people couldn't find the companies they wanted.","Man: I'll design a map and put it on the event app, as well as printing a few copies at the entrance.","Man: Excellent. Finally, let's send a feedback survey to companies and students afterwards, so we can improve next year."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M28-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why does the second woman think the first woman's experience will be useful?","o":["The new team works on solar-powered desalination.","The new team needs a manager.","Solar energy is similar to chemistry.","The team leader is her friend."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"The fields are closely connected.","h":"ارتباط المجالين."},{"n":84,"s":"What does the second woman advise her to prepare before leaving?","o":["a list of new colleagues","a presentation about solar energy","a request for a higher salary","a handover document for her current project Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'prepare a clear handover document'.","h":"وثيقة تسليم."}],"t":"Planning a move to a new research team","sc":["Situation: A researcher talks to a colleague about a job change. (180 words)","Woman: I've been offered a position in the renewable energy research team. I'm excited, but also a bit nervous.","Woman: Congratulations! What's worrying you?","Woman: Well, I've worked on water desalination for five years. Solar energy is a different field, and I'll have to learn a lot quickly.","Woman: Actually, the two fields are closely connected. The team is developing solar-powered desalination, isn't it? Your experience will be very valuable.","Woman: That's true. I hadn't thought of it that way.","Woman: Before you start, ask the team leader for a list of key articles to read. And try to meet each team member individually during your first week.","Woman: Good advice. I'm also worried about leaving my current project unfinished.","Woman: Then prepare a clear handover document, explaining where everything is and what still needs to be done. Your replacement will appreciate it.","Woman: I'll start writing it this week. I want to leave on good terms.","Woman: You will. And remember, you can always contact your old team if you need advice. Moving between projects is normal in research.","Woman: Thank you. I feel much more confident about it now."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M28-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why was sailing north on the Red Sea difficult?","o":["The water was too shallow everywhere.","There were no ports in the north.","Strong winds blew from the north for most of the year.","Pirates controlled the northern coast."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الرياح الشمالية."},{"n":86,"s":"Why did Jeddah become especially important after the seventh century?","o":["It was the only port with no reefs.","It was the largest coffee producer.","It became the port for Makkah.","It was connected to the Suez Canal."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"ميناء مكة."},{"n":87,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention the name 'Mokha'?","o":["to show how famous the port was for exporting coffee","to describe a type of ship","to explain the monsoon winds","to compare it with Suez"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Its name is still used for coffee.","h":"شهرته في تصدير القهوة."}],"t":"Extended lecture: how ports shaped the Red Sea economy","sc":["Situation: A history lecturer speaks to students. (311 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we'll examine the role of ports in the history of the Red Sea. The Red Sea is a narrow body of water, about two thousand two hundred kilometres long, linking the Mediterranean world, via Egypt, with the Indian Ocean. For thousands of years, it has been one of the world's most important trade routes, and its ports have shaped the economies of the surrounding lands. Sailing on the Red Sea was never easy. Its northern part has strong winds that blow from the north for most of the year, making it difficult for ships to sail north. Coral reefs line much of the coast, creating dangers for ships. As a result, ancient and medieval sailors relied on detailed local knowledge, and ports developed where there were natural harbours with safe access through the reefs. Trade followed the rhythm of the monsoon winds of the Indian Ocean, which change direction with the seasons. Ships from India and East Africa arrived at certain times of year, so ports had busy and quiet seasons. On the Arabian side, Jeddah became especially important after the seventh century, when it was chosen as the port for Makkah. Pilgrims arriving by sea, as well as goods such as spices, textiles and coffee, passed through it, and the city's merchants grew wealthy. Further south, the Yemeni port of Mokha became famous in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries as the main export centre for coffee, so famous, in fact, that its name is still used for a type of coffee today. The opening of the Suez Canal in 1869 transformed the region, allowing ships to sail directly from Europe to Asia through the Red Sea. Today, the Red Sea carries about a tenth of global trade, and Saudi Arabia is investing heavily in ports, logistics zones and new coastal cities to benefit from this strategic location."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M28-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"How can satellites detect unhealthy plants?","o":["Unhealthy plants become warmer.","Unhealthy plants reflect less near-infrared light.","Satellites can hear plants.","Unhealthy plants change their shape."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Explained in the lecture.","h":"انعكاس الأشعة تحت الحمراء."},{"n":89,"s":"Why is precision agriculture particularly valuable in Saudi Arabia?","o":["Saudi farms are very cloudy.","Saudi Arabia has no satellites.","Farmers there use no fertilizer.","Groundwater is limited and farming uses much water."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"محدودية المياه الجوفية."},{"n":90,"s":"Which limitation of satellite technology does the lecturer mention?","o":["Satellites cannot measure moisture.","The data is never free.","GPS does not work on farms.","Clouds can block optical images."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الغيوم تحجب الصور."}],"t":"Lecture: how satellites help farmers","sc":["Situation: An agriculture lecturer speaks to students. (288 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Today, I'd like to talk about how satellites are changing farming, an approach often called precision agriculture. Traditionally, farmers treated an entire field in the same way, applying the same amount of water, fertilizer and pesticide everywhere. But fields are rarely uniform. Soil quality, moisture and plant health can vary considerably from one part of a field to another. Satellites can help farmers see these differences. Satellites carry sensors that measure not only visible light but also infrared light, which our eyes cannot see. Healthy plants reflect a lot of near-infrared light, while stressed or diseased plants reflect less. By comparing different wavelengths, scientists can calculate an index of vegetation health, which shows which areas of a field are growing well and which are struggling, sometimes before any problem is visible from the ground. Satellites can also estimate soil moisture and surface temperature. With this information, farmers can apply water and fertilizer only where they are needed, saving money and reducing environmental damage. This is particularly valuable in dry regions like Saudi Arabia, where groundwater is limited and agriculture uses a large share of water resources. Satellite navigation also plays a role: tractors guided by GPS can follow precise routes, avoiding overlaps and gaps when planting or spraying. In addition, satellite data helps governments monitor crop production over large areas, predict harvests and detect early signs of drought, which supports planning for food security. There are some limitations. Clouds can block optical satellite images, although radar satellites can see through them, and small farms may find the technology expensive or complicated. However, as more satellite data becomes freely available and smartphone apps make it easier to interpret, these tools are spreading rapidly, even among small-scale farmers."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 3 – 4 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) As a result, the number of visitors doubled within a year.","(2) The old museum had few visitors and outdated displays.","(3) The management decided to introduce interactive exhibits and evening events.","(4) They also launched a social media campaign aimed at young people."],"k":0,"ty":"best order","e":"problem → action → further action → result.","h":"مشكلة ثم إجراءات ثم نتيجة."},{"n":92,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["As a result","On the other hand","For example","Therefore"],"x":["Some students prefer studying alone. ______, others learn better in groups, where they can discuss ideas."],"k":1,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence contrasts with the first.","h":"On the other hand للمقابلة."},{"n":93,"s":"(A) Many of the (B) information on the website (C) is (D) outdated.","o":["Many","information","is","outdated"],"k":0,"ty":"error","e":"information is uncountable: Much.","h":"Much مع غير المعدود."},{"n":94,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) Next, the seeds are planted in rows about thirty centimetres apart.","(2) Before planting, farmers prepare the soil by removing stones and weeds.","(3) They then add compost to improve the soil's fertility.","(4) Finally, the field is watered regularly until the plants appear."],"k":2,"ty":"best order","e":"Before → then → Next → Finally.","h":"ترتيب الخطوات."},{"n":95,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Nevertheless","In contrast","Otherwise","Consequently"],"x":["The company invested heavily in staff training. ______, customer satisfaction improved significantly."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence is a result.","h":"Consequently للنتيجة."},{"n":96,"s":"The students (A) were asked (B) to (C) writing a short (D) essay.","o":["were","to","writing","essay"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"ask someone to + base verb: write.","h":"to + المصدر."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Al-Ula is home to remarkable archaeological sites. (2) The ancient city of Hegra contains more than a hundred tombs carved into rock. (3) Dadan was the capital of an ancient kingdom. (4) Many people enjoy shopping in modern malls. (5) Rock inscriptions in the area date back thousands of years."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Shopping malls do not develop the point about archaeology.","h":"المراكز التجارية خارج الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Budgeting apps can help people manage their money more effectively.","Many people own smartphones.","Bills arrive every month.","Banks open at eight o'clock."],"x":["______ They can track their spending, set savings goals and receive alerts when bills are due. Some apps even give advice on how to reduce unnecessary expenses."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe how the apps help.","h":"تطبيقات الميزانية."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Deserts cover large areas of Arabia.","Some hikers prefer mountains.","Water bottles can be heavy.","With the right preparation, hikers can enjoy the desert safely."],"x":["Desert hiking can be a wonderful experience, but it requires careful preparation. Hikers should carry plenty of water, wear protective clothing and avoid walking during the hottest part of the day. They should also tell someone where they are going. ______"],"k":3,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the advice.","h":"خلاصة النصائح."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some universities are very old.","They can study at any time and from any place.","Computers need electricity.","Teachers mark many essays. Teacher Section — Mock 28"],"x":["Online learning gives students great flexibility. ______ However, it also requires a high level of self-discipline."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"It explains flexibility before 'However'.","h":"توضيح المرونة."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
